## B5. The Hyperblock engine (real design, real math)

### Storage: block-hybrid, not pure CSF
Pure CSF is elegant but wrong for FP&A's access patterns (you need random cell writes and fast slice iteration). Use **blocks with adaptive encoding**:

```
Module = ordered dimension list + line items
Space partitioned into blocks of 4096 cells along the fastest-varying dims
Block encodings (chosen automatically, re-chosen on write pressure):
  EMPTY      → 0 bytes                     (99% of most models)
  CONSTANT   → 1 value + validity          (spread/allocated values — huge win)
  DENSE      → 4096 × 8B + 512B bitmap     (32.5 KB)
  SPARSE     → sorted (u16 local_idx, i64 value) pairs
  DICT       → small value set + index array (status flags, categories)
Per-block metadata: sum, min, max, count, dirty_bits, last_write_seq
```

**Honest memory math** (replace v1's "100 billion intersections in a few MB"):

| Model | Populated cells | Engine working set | Total app RAM |
|---|---|---|---|
| Small (dept budget) | 500 K | ~25 MB | ~600 MB |
| Medium (company plan) | 10 M | ~220 MB | ~1.1 GB |
| Large (multi-entity, SKU-level) | 100 M | ~2.2 GB | ~4 GB |
| XLarge (GL-transaction grain) | 1 B+ | mmap + DuckDB spill, hot set cached | 8 GB+ |

Rule of thumb: **~12–22 bytes per populated cell** including indexes, aggregates, and DAG. Dimension *intersections* are free (EMPTY blocks); **populated cells cost memory**. Publish this table in your docs — it builds trust.

### Aggregation: incremental delta propagation
- Precomputed aggregate trees per hierarchy. Leaf change → propagate `delta = new − old` to ancestors. **O(depth)**, not O(subtree).
- **Non-additive measures are first-class**: `SUM`, `PRODUCT`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, `AVG`, `WEIGHTED_AVG(w)`, `FIRST`, `LAST` (semi-additive balances!), `OPENING`, `CLOSING`, `NONE`.
- **Alternate hierarchies** = separate trees over the same leaves (management vs statutory vs tax). Ragged hierarchies supported natively.
- **Effective-dated hierarchies (SCD2)** — *the #1 thing v1 missed*:
  ```
  hierarchy_edge(parent, child, weight, valid_from, valid_to, hierarchy_id)
  ```
  Every rollup takes an `as_of` date. Reorgs mid-year must not rewrite history. Restatements create new versions, never mutate closed actuals.

### Dependency graph: line-item level, not cell level
**Cell-level actors/DAG nodes will kill you** (100 M nodes = death). Design:
- **Node = line item × dimensional slice signature** (typically 10 K–500 K nodes).
- Static dimensional analysis determines which slices a formula touches. Compile to vectorized kernels over blocks.
- Fall back to cell-level nodes **only** for self-referential/time-lagged formulas (`PREVIOUS`, recursive allocations) — and even then, per-block, not per-cell.
- **SCC detection (Tarjan)** at compile time → classify:
  - **Not a cycle**: `Cash_Ending = PREVIOUS(Cash_Ending) + NCF` — time-lagged, resolves by period ordering. (v1 mislabeled this as circular.)
  - **True simultaneity**: interest ↔ debt ↔ cash ↔ net income → iterative solver.
- **Deterministic scheduler**: topological levels, parallel within level, fixed order within block. Cancellable, progress-reporting, time-sliced so UI never freezes.

### Circular reference solver
```
method: Gauss-Seidel with optional damping λ ∈ (0,1]
order:  FIXED canonical order (sorted by node_id) — reproducibility > speed
tol:    abs 1e-6 money units AND rel 1e-9 (both must pass)
max_iter: 100 (configurable per SCC)
on_divergence: stop, report SCC members + residual trajectory, mark cells #CIRC!
UI: "Circularity Report" panel with member list, convergence chart, and
    one-click "break with average-balance convention"
```
Also ship the **average-balance interest convention** (`interest = rate × (opening+closing)/2`) as a modeling option — it eliminates most intentional circularity legitimately.

### Allocation engine (a real differentiator)
- Methods: proportional to driver, historical curve, equal, manual weights, rank-based, capacity-constrained.
- **Cascading & reciprocal allocations** (step-down, simultaneous-equation / reciprocal method) with iteration + convergence reporting.
- **Penny-difference resolution**: largest-remainder method so allocations sum **exactly** to the source. Every FP&A tool that gets this wrong loses controllers permanently. Make it a tested invariant.
- **Full allocation trace**: source → driver → weight → target, exportable. Auditors love this.

## B6. Formula language spec (give the AI a real grammar)

**Design principle:** Excel-familiar surface, Anaplan-grade dimensional semantics, **total functions** (no runtime panics — typed error propagation).

```ebnf
expr        = ternary ;
ternary     = or_expr [ "?" expr ":" expr ] | "IF" expr "THEN" expr "ELSE" expr ;
or_expr     = and_expr { ("OR"|"||") and_expr } ;
and_expr    = cmp { ("AND"|"&&") cmp } ;
cmp         = add [ ("="|"!="|"<"|"<="|">"|">=") add ] ;
add         = mul { ("+"|"-") mul } ;
mul         = unary { ("*"|"/") unary } ;
unary       = ["-"|"NOT"] postfix ;
postfix     = primary { "[" scope "]" | "." member | "(" args ")" } ;
scope       = ("SUM"|"ANY"|"ALL"|"FIRST"|"LAST"|"AVG"|"WAVG") ":" dim_expr
            | "SELECT:" dim_expr
            | "LOOKUP:" list_expr
            | "BY:" dim_list ;
primary     = number | money | string | date | bool | BLANK
            | line_item_ref | func_call | "(" expr ")" ;
```

**Semantics table (must be in the spec, or AI will invent inconsistent behavior):**

| Construct | Semantics | Trap to avoid |
|---|---|---|
| `A * B` | Intersection-wise; missing intersection → BLANK (not 0) | Treating BLANK as 0 silently |
| `A / B` | **Ratio of sums** when aggregating, unless annotated `RATIO_OF_RATIOS` | Sum-of-ratios bug — the classic FP&A error |
| `X[SUM: Dept]` | Aggregate away Dept, broadcast result | Wrong broadcast direction |
| `X[SELECT: Dept.All]` | Pick a specific member, no aggregation | Confusing SELECT with SUM |
| `X[LOOKUP: Product.Category]` | Map via property/list; requires a mapping relation | Many-to-one ambiguity must error at compile time |
| `PREVIOUS(X)` / `NEXT(X)` | Time-lagged; first period → `initial` arg or BLANK | Creating false circularity |
| `CUMULATE(X)`, `MOVINGSUM(X,n)`, `MOVINGAVG(X,n)` | Window functions over Time with calendar awareness | Fiscal vs calendar confusion |
| `YTD`, `QTD`, `TTM`, `PRIOR_YEAR`, `YOY` | Calendar-aware, fiscal-year aware | 53-week years |
| `SPREAD(src, driver)`, `ALLOCATE(...)` | Exact-sum allocation with remainder resolution | Penny drift |
| `COPY(version)` | Version-aware read | Cross-version leakage |
| `RANK`, `PERCENTILE`, `ROUND(x, n, mode)` | Explicit rounding mode required for money | Implicit banker's rounding |
| BLANK propagation | Arithmetic: BLANK propagates. Aggregation: BLANK skipped. Explicit `COALESCE`, `ISBLANK` | Ambiguity |
| Errors | `#DIM!`, `#TYPE!`, `#REF!`, `#CIRC!`, `#DIV0!`, `#CONV!` — typed, propagate, never panic | Crashing the engine |

**Single Function Registry** (huge AI leverage): one declarative table drives → parser, type checker, evaluator, Excel XLL UDF list, documentation, **and the LLM tool schema**. Adding a function = one registry entry + tests; everything else generates.

```yaml
- name: MOVINGAVG
  args: [{series: Numeric, periods: Int, include_current: Bool = true}]
  returns: Numeric
  dims: {requires: [Time], preserves: "*"}
  null_policy: skip_blanks
  money_safe: true
  excel_name: FPA.MOVINGAVG
  doc: "Rolling mean over n fiscal periods."
  examples: [{expr: "MOVINGAVG(Gross_Margin, 3)", expect: "..."}]
